61:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="1">On October 2, 2026, G7 leaders agreed to a coordinated release of 100 million barrels through the International Energy Agency "to begin immediately over 4 months", "taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled", with what they called a "frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days". The same statement says the group will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns. It reaffirms a commitment to refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="We will coordinate maintenance schedules across G7 refineries to prevent simultaneous capacity shutdowns" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="refrain from export restrictions on energy and energy products between G7 countries" /></p>
62:        <p data-layer="narrative">The French presidency's readout states a ceiling, "jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois", up to 100 million barrels within 4 months. That readout names refined products, notably diesel and crude.<Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /><Cite s="elysee-g7-readout-2026-10-02" passage="notamment de diesel et de pétrole brut, jusqu'à 100 millions de barils sous 4 mois" /></p>
63:        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="5">In March, International Energy Agency members pledged 400 million barrels; by October 2 around 325 million had been released, and that day the G7 set 100 million barrels over 4 months without saying how much of it is beyond the March pledge. The October 2 statement asks the agency to "monitor the immediate and full implementation of the March 2026 commitments".<Cite s="doe-spr-release-2026-03-11" passage="coordinated release of 400 million barrels" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="monitor the immediate and full implementation of the March 2026 commitments" /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
66:        <p data-layer="narrative">The October 2 statement calls for the immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz. Net exports of diesel/gasoil from the Gulf countries averaged 390,000 barrels a day in August, just over a quarter of levels before the war in the Middle East. Combined, net exports of diesel/gasoil exports from the Gulf and Russia in August were 1.6 million barrels a day lower than in February.<Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="immediate and full restoration of navigational rights and principles in the Strait of Hormuz" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="averaged 390 kb/d in August, just over a quarter of pre-war levels" /><Cite s="iea-omr-2026-09" passage="Combined, net exports of diesel/gasoil exports from the Gulf and Russia in August were 1.6 mb/d lower than in February" /></p>
97:          <DataTable columns={["Record", "Wording of the 100 million barrels"]} rows={statementRows} />
107:              <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="3">President Donald Trump posted that Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil and that the process will begin immediately. The joint statement gives no number for the diesel, names no country that will supply it, and records no barrel delivered; it calls the diesel release "substantial" and places it in the first 20 days.<Cite s="trumpstruth-post-2026-10-02" passage="Europe has just agreed to release a massive amount of their heavily stocked Diesel Oil. The process will begin immediately." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /></p>
113:              <h3>100 million barrels of diesel in 20 days, or 50 and 50</h3>
114:              <p data-layer="narrative">Reuters reported a demand to "release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window". A Friday, October 2, proposal, before the statement, said European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil.<Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of diesel within a 20-day window" /><Cite s="reuters-eu-proposal-2026-10-02" passage="European countries would release 50 million barrels of diesel and International Energy Agency members would release 50 million barrels of crude oil" /></p>
117:              <h3>100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel</h3>
118:              <p data-layer="narrative">The Wall Street Journal's lede said the countries would "release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks". IEA Executive Director Fatih Birol noted that around {marchSoFar.barrels} million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over {marchSoFar.share} of the {release.marchTotal} barrels originally pledged in the action.<Cite s="wsj-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="release 100 million barrels of crude oil and fuel from their emergency stocks" /><Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /></p>
162:          <p>Welt reported on Friday evening that the federal government welcomed the release of 100 million barrels of oil and products such as diesel.<Cite s="welt-g7-welcome-2026-10-02" passage="Auch die Bundesregierung hat den G-7-Beschluss zur Freigabe von 100 Millionen Barrel Öl und Ölprodukten wie Diesel als wichtiges Zeichen für die Handlungsfähigkeit und Geschlossenheit der Gruppe begrüßt." /></p>
231:        <p data-layer="narrative">The IEA's October 2 account puts the {marchSoFar.barrels} million barrels on the March collective action. The October statement does not assign that tally to a portion of its own {release.barrels} barrel timetable. France 24 said 80 percent of overall IEA stocks were "still in our pocket", which is overall stocks, not the unreleased share of the March 400. The German FAQ says the IEA had asked Germany to release 2,6 million tonnes from its reserve, a March request. RTÉ reported that US oil prices dropped sharply, as much as 5% at one point. The diesel series' last row is September 28, and Brent's last row is September 29.<Cite s="iea-g7-meeting-2026-10-02" passage="He noted that around 325 million barrels of the IEA collective action announced on 11 March have so far been released, representing over 80% of the 400 million barrels originally pledged in the action." /><Cite s="govuk-g7-statement-2026-10-02" passage="taking into account commitments that have already been fulfilled, we will implement our commitments with a coordinated release through the IEA of 100 million barrels (MB) to begin immediately over 4 months, including a frontloaded substantial diesel release within the first 20 days" /><Cite s="france24-g7-release-2026-10-02" passage="80 percent of overall IEA stocks “still in our pocket”" /><Cite s="bundesregierung-energy-faq-2026-10-01" passage="Die IEA hatte Deutschland gebeten, 2,6 Millionen Tonnen aus seiner Reserve freizugeben." /><Cite s="rte-von-der-leyen-2026-10-02" passage="US oil prices dropped sharply - as much as 5% at one point" /><Cite s="gasdesw-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-28,6.382" /><Cite s="dcoilbrenteu-2026-10-03" passage="2026-09-29,113.96" /></p>

